    <h2>Why developers switch from Uptime Kuma to PingWatch</h2>
    <p>The most common reason is maintenance fatigue. Running Uptime Kuma means your monitoring depends on a server you also have to keep running. If the VPS goes down, your monitor goes down with it. You end up monitoring your monitor.</p>
    <p>PingWatch runs in the cloud so you never have to think about it. When your site goes down, you get an alert. When it comes back, you get another one — with how long it was down. That is all most developers actually need.</p>

    <h2>Downtime reason in every alert</h2>
    <p>One thing PingWatch does differently from Uptime Kuma: when your site goes down, the alert includes a short explanation of why — for example, a timeout, a DNS failure, or a specific HTTP error code. You do not have to log in and investigate to understand what happened. The information arrives in the alert itself.</p>

    <h2>Who PingWatch is for</h2>
    <p>PingWatch is built for developers and founders who want monitoring that simply works — without becoming a maintenance project of its own. It is a good fit for:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Indie developers with one or more side projects</li>
      <li>Early-stage startups and solo founders</li>
      <li>Small teams who do not have a DevOps engineer</li>
      <li>Anyone who tried Uptime Kuma and found the setup too heavy</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How to switch from Uptime Kuma to PingWatch</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Create a free PingWatch account at pingwatch.org.</li>
      <li>Choose your alert channel — Telegram, Discord, Slack, or Email.</li>
      <li>Add the URLs you were monitoring in Uptime Kuma.</li>
      <li>Done. PingWatch starts checking them every 5 minutes immediately.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>You can keep Uptime Kuma running in parallel for as long as you want. Most people find they stop needing it within a few days.</p>

    <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>

    <h3>Does PingWatch check every 5 minutes?</h3>
    <p>Yes. PingWatch pings your URL every 5 minutes, around the clock, automatically.</p>

    <h3>What happens when my site comes back online?</h3>
    <p>You receive a recovery alert with the total downtime duration in minutes, so you know exactly how long the incident lasted.</p>

    <h3>Can I monitor APIs, not just websites?</h3>
    <p>Yes. Any URL that returns an HTTP response can be monitored — websites, REST APIs, health check endpoints, and webhooks.</p>

    <h3>Is there a free plan?</h3>
